.TH MAP_PHYS 2
.SH NAME
map_phys \- map a device's registers or memory, for drivers (SYS_MAP_PHYS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

void *map_phys(uint64_t pa, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B map_phys
maps
.I size
bytes of physical address space from
.I pa
into the caller, uncached, and returns where. It is how a driver reaches
its device's registers, or memory like a frame buffer. The mapping lasts
as long as the process.
.PP
.I size
may be or-ed with
.BR MAP_WC :
memory a device only reads, such as a frame buffer, where writes may be
gathered and reordered (much faster drawing on arm64; x86-64 maps it
uncached as usual).
.PP
Never RAM, which would let a driver read anyone's memory (the frame
buffer the firmware set up is allowed), and never the interrupt
controllers, which are the kernel's. Drivers only.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The address of
.I pa
in the caller's memory, or a negative error (cast to a pointer).
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not a driver, or the range is RAM or the kernel's.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I size
is 0 or above 1 GiB, or the range wraps around.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No memory for the page tables.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dma_alloc (2),
.BR irq_bind (2),
.BR sys_screen (2)
